;; Docs for std/monad.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "monad.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "The Monad trait, its instances for List, Option and Result, and the (do ...) block.")
  (see pure bind do)
  (example """
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (std monad))

    (: calc (-> int (Option int)))
    (defun (calc n)
      (do (:bind half (safe-div n 2))
          (:let  doubled (* half 2))
          (:bind third (safe-div doubled 3))
          (:return (+ doubled third))))
    """)
  (reference """
    A monad is a type with two operations: @code{pure} puts a value in it,
    and @code{bind} gives the value inside it to a function that returns
    the next one. A @code{(do ...)} block writes a chain of @code{bind}
    calls as a list of steps.

    @strong{The instances.}

    @itemlist{
      @item{@code{List}: @code{bind} applies the function to each item and
        appends the lists that it returns. So a block runs once for each
        combination of items.}
      @item{@code{Option}: @code{bind} stops at @code{None}. The block
        returns @code{None} when a step returns @code{None}.}
      @item{@code{Result}: @code{bind} stops at @code{Err}. The block
        returns the first @code{Err}, and the steps after it do not run.}
    }

    The type of the block selects the instance, as for any trait method.
    """))

(:doc Monad
  (summary "The trait for a type that can chain computations: pure and bind.")
  (see pure bind do)
  (reference """
    An implementation gives @code{pure} and @code{bind} for a type
    constructor, such as @code{List} or @code{Option}. A @code{(do ...)}
    block then works for that type.
    """))

(:doc pure
  (summary "Puts a value in the monad.")
  (arg x "The value.")
  (returns "The monad that holds only x: (Cons x Nil), (Some x) or (Ok x).")
  (see bind do))

(:doc bind
  (summary "Gives the value in a monad to a function that returns the next monad.")
  (arg m "The monad.")
  (arg k "The function. It gets each value in m, and may be of either colour.")
  (returns "The result of k. For List, the results for all items, appended in order. For None or Err, m's None or Err, and k is not called.")
  (see pure do)
  (example """
    (bind (Some 2) (fun (x) (Some (* x 10))))   ; (Some 20)
    (bind (list 1 2) (fun (x) (list x x)))      ; (1 1 2 2)
    """))

(:doc do
  (summary "Writes a chain of bind calls as a block of steps.")
  (form (do step ...))
  (rest step """
    A step, one of:

    @code{(:bind name expr)}: binds @code{name} to the value in the monad
    @code{expr}. @code{name} is a plain identifier.

    @code{(:match pattern expr)}: as @code{:bind}, but matches the value
    against @code{pattern}. A value that does not match raises an
    exception, as in @code{match}.

    @code{(:let name expr)}: binds @code{name} to @code{expr} itself, with
    no @code{bind}.

    @code{(:then expr)}: binds the monad @code{expr} and ignores its value.

    @code{(:return expr)}: the last step only. The block returns
    @code{(pure expr)}.

    Any other form: evaluated for its side effects, with no @code{bind}.
    As the last step, it is the value of the block, and must be a monad.
    """)
  (see bind pure)
  (example """
    ;; All sums of one item from each list.
    (do (:bind x (list 1 2))
        (:bind y (list 10 20))
        (:return (+ x y)))          ; (11 21 12 22)

    ;; Stops at the first Err.
    (do (:match (Tuple w h) (parse-pair text))
        (:bind width (checked w))
        (:bind height (checked h))
        (:return (* width height)))
    """)
  (reference """
    The block needs at least one step, and its last step is its value: a
    @code{:return}, or a form that returns a monad. A @code{:bind},
    @code{:match}, @code{:let} or @code{:then} can not be last. A
    keyword-headed form that is not one of these steps is a syntax error.

    The last step can be a call that returns a monad, so a monadic loop can
    put its recursive call in tail position.

    The steps after a @code{:bind}, @code{:match} or @code{:then} are the
    body of a @code{fun} that is given to @code{bind}. So they can not
    suspend.
    """))
